      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rock Island takes its name from a rock island on the Caney Fork River just below where the Rocky River joins it.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Island%2C_Tennessee",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Long before settlers, the Cherokee and Chickamauga people used the area around Rock Island as a vital travel corridor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_island_tennessee",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "When the railroad arrived in 1881, residents moved the village from the river confluence to a new spot along the tracks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://tennesseeencyclopedia.net/entries/rock-island-state-park/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Great Falls Dam, finished in 1916, stretches 800 feet across the Caney Fork and towers 92 feet high.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rock_island_tennessee",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
